                            <p></p><h3>Definition of "tk12":</h3><p><b>TK-12</b> is one of Telefunken Elektroakustik&#8217;s <em>two</em> modern recreations of <a href="http://recordinghacks.com/microphones/tag/ck12">AKG&#8217;s CK-12</a> capsule. </p><p>The company, until April 2008, manufactured both the TK-12 and a &#8220;CK-12;&#8221; the latter is claimed to be an exact replica of the original brass-ring capsule, discontinued by AKG sometime in the mid-1970s (as suggested by the initial appearance of the Teflon CK12 in the C414EB, which was introduced in 1976).</p><p>In contrast, the TK-12 uses &#8220;modern techniques and standard parts (molded plastics and assembly screws)&#8221; that allow the company to &#8220;streamline the manufacturing process&#8221; (according to the company <a href="http://t-funk.com/about/faq.php">FAQ</a>) and presumably reduce costs.</p><p>The TK-12 is a large-diaphragm condenser capsule with dual 10-micron gold-sputtered Mylar diaphragms.</p><p>To confuse matters, the company&#8217;s Ela M 12 / C12 microphone has at times been advertised with the CK-12, and at times with the TK-12. It is unknown whether the the company intentionally initially referred to their CK-12 reproduction &#8212; described in 2004 as &#8220;an exact reproduction of the CK-12 capsule found in historic Telefunken 251 microphones&#8221; &#8212; as the TK-12.</p>
